From: TechNet <local>
Subject: [PATCH] app-tracker: Keep the pid DBus gave us for an app we launched

Launching from the app grid creates the tracker state before anything knows a
pid, so phosh_app_state_new is called with 0. The pid arrives moments later on
org.gtk.gio.DesktopAppInfo's Launched signal -- which on_dbus_app_launched
parses, and then throws away, passing a literal 0 to update_app_state for the
case where the startup id is already known.

update_app_state would have taken it:

    if (state->pid && pid && state->pid != pid)
      g_warning ("Ignoring pid change ...");
    else if (pid)
      state->pid = pid;

so the state keeps pid == 0 for the whole launch, and that decides how long the
app is given. on_startup_timeout only extends the deadline when it has a pid to
check:

    if (state->pid && state->timeout.waited < STARTUP_TRACKED_TIMEOUT)

With no pid the first tick declares the launch failed, at STARTUP_TIMEOUT, five
seconds. With one it waits up to STARTUP_TRACKED_TIMEOUT, twenty-seven.

Five seconds is not long on a phone. Measured on a PinePhone, Files takes 14.7s
warm and 21.8s cold, and Epiphany 8.6s -- so every one of them draws a spurious
"failed to start" notification, has its splash torn down, and then appears
anyway a few seconds later, because nothing actually went wrong. 26 of these
were logged in a single boot.

Passing the pid through puts those launches on the 27s deadline they were meant
to have.

--- a/src/app-tracker.c
+++ b/src/app-tracker.c
@@ -513,7 +513,7 @@
   if (g_hash_table_contains (self->apps, startup_id)) {
     /* App already known, likely launched by us */
     g_debug ("'%s' (%s) already known", startup_id, desktop_file);
-    update_app_state (self, startup_id, PHOSH_APP_TRACKER_STATE_FLAG_DBUS_LAUNCH, 0);
+    update_app_state (self, startup_id, PHOSH_APP_TRACKER_STATE_FLAG_DBUS_LAUNCH, pid);
   } else {
     GDesktopAppInfo *info;
     PhoshAppState *state;
